  "configuration_id": "config_002",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_044 (RNA-only, scgpt, multiprofile, avgpool) achieves 0.530 acc / 0.515 F1, outperforming config_006 (multimodal, same RNA/image/aggregation, concat fusion) at 0.512 acc / 0.477 F1. This suggests the image modality, as fused via simple concatenation, is adding noise rather than signal. Question: config_002 is a single-variable change from config_006—only the fusion switches from concat to weighted—keeping scgpt, resnet50, avgpool, and multiprofile identical. This isolates whether the fusion mechanism is the bottleneck: weighted fusion lets the model learn to down-weight unhelpful image features, whereas concat forces equal treatment. Why best next: it is the most direct, minimal-difference follow-up to the key finding (RNA > multimodal), cleanly attributing any performance shift to the fusion choice. If weighted fusion recovers the gap, the image carries useful signal that concat was masking; if it does not, the evidence strengthens that resnet50 image features are not informative under this setup, redirecting budget toward other design axes (encoder, aggregation, or profile setting)."
}

